-- Calling, from another module, a rule that reads a section GIVEN.
--
-- The call below passes one argument, which is the arity written in the file
-- that defines `tax on`. That rule gained a second, trailing input when its own
-- module was discharged. Nothing bridges the two -- discharge does not cross
-- IMPORT -- so the evaluator binds the missing input from the imported module's
-- own cell, which is where the rule would have read it before discharge.
--
-- Without that, this file used to stop with
-- "Internal error: given signatures' values' lengths do not match".
--
-- What this does NOT give you: supplying `the rate` from here. The name itself
-- IS in scope -- `IMPORT` brings it in like any other, and a rule here that
-- merely READS it answers 0.05 (measured 2026-09-08; an earlier version of this
-- comment said the name was out of scope, which is wrong). What does not cross
-- is the SUPPLY: `CheckEnv.sectionBinderNames` is reset at the import boundary,
-- so a `WITH` naming it is refused, and the imported file's own TYPICALLY is
-- what applies -- 0.05, hence 5 rather than anything this file could choose.
--
-- Nor may a rule here be `@export`ed if it reaches a rule that reads it: that
-- is the §11.19 refusal, pinned by `not-ok/import/export-refused.l4`. This file
-- has no `@export`, which is exactly why it stays legal.

IMPORT `section-given-import-def`

GIVETH A NUMBER
`tax on a hundred` MEANS `tax on` 100

#EVAL `tax on a hundred`
#ASSERT `tax on a hundred` EQUALS 5
